  "summary": "A self-propelled crawler drill rig drills the blast holes that break the hard ferricrete caprock (and any hard ore). The feed (mast) is raised to vertical, the hydraulic rock drill (top hammer — or a down-the-hole hammer on larger rigs) hammers and rotates the drill string into the rock while the screw compressor blows flushing air down the rods that carries the cuttings up to the collar hood and dust collector. Holes of 89–127 mm on a ~3.0 × 3.5 m pattern are then charged with bulk emulsion/ANFO.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Mining — drill & blast",
    "type": "Crawler surface drill rig, top-hammer/DTH, 89–127 mm"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "scale": "Real size (metres)",
    "animation": "Feed tilted forward from the tramming position to the design angle (here vertical), foot pressed down, rock drill drills ~3 m (time-compressed ≈ 30×; real penetration ≈ 0.5–1.5 m/min), string withdrawn while flushing, foot lifted, feed back to the tramming position (12 s loop)",
    "basis": "Generic surface top-hammer rig class (e.g. 20–25 t class); reference-plant design basis A01"
  },
